---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Tankless Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-tankless-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "What Size Tankless Water Heater Do I Need?",
    "url": "/what-size-tankless-water-heater-do-i-need/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Tankless Water Heater Cost",
    "url": "/tankless-water-heater-cost/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Condensing vs Non-Condensing Tankless",
    "url": "/condensing-vs-non-condensing-tankless-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Tankless Water Heater Not Heating",
    "url": "/tankless-water-heater-not-heating/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Heat Pump Water Heaters",
    "url": "/best-heat-pump-water-heater/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-06", modified: "2026-10-06" });
const title = "6 Best Electric Tankless Water Heaters in 2026: Sized for Your Panel";
const description =
  "Six electric tankless water heaters compared on real GPM by groundwater temperature, breakers and total amps, so you know what your house and panel can run.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "stiebel-eltron-tempra-24",
    "name": "Stiebel Eltron Tempra 24 Plus",
    "category": "Whole-House Electric Tankless, 24 kW",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 90,
    "pros": [
      "Advanced Flow Control trims flow instead of letting the temperature sag when demand exceeds capacity",
      "Needs 2 x 50 A double-pole breakers (100 A at 240 V), less panel capacity than most whole-house units",
      "Stiebel's chart shows about 4.7 GPM at 70°F inlet and 3.0 GPM at 50°F, with 105°F output",
      "Digital display with temperature presets, a flow-rate readout and an energy monitor",
      "7-year leakage and 3-year parts warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About 2.5 GPM at 40°F inlet, which is one shower in a northern winter",
      "Costs more than EcoSmart or Rheem units with similar kW"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The electric tankless we would put in a one- or two-bathroom house in a moderate or warm climate, because it handles overload more gracefully than anything else here.",
    "description": "The Tempra 24 Plus earns the top spot for one feature: Advanced Flow Control. Every electric tankless has a hard ceiling set by its kilowatts, and when you open more taps than it can heat, most units simply let the outlet temperature fall. The Plus models use a motorized valve to reduce flow instead, so the shower stays hot but runs a little weaker. That is the right failure mode for a house where two people sometimes shower at once. Electrically it is also one of the easier whole-house units to live with. Stiebel Eltron specifies two 50 amp double-pole breakers, a 100 amp draw at 240 volts, and recommends at least 150 amp household service, which many homes already have. The capacity limit is the honest catch. Stiebel's own chart shows about 4.7 GPM at 70°F incoming water but only 2.5 GPM at 40°F, so in Minnesota in February this is a one-shower heater. The adjustable range runs from 68°F to 140°F, and the warranty covers leakage for seven years and parts for three. If your groundwater is cold or you need two showers at once, step up to the Tempra 29 Plus."
  },
  {
    "id": "eemax-ex280t2t",
    "name": "EcoSmart ECO 27",
    "category": "Whole-House Electric Tankless, 27 kW",
    "badge": "Best Value Whole-House",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.1,
    "reviewCount": 10,
    "pros": [
      "27 kW of self-modulating heat from three 9 kW elements",
      "EcoSmart's chart shows 5.2 GPM at 70°F inlet and 3.4 GPM at 50°F, with 105°F output",
      "Low 0.3 GPM activation flow, so a slow trickle at a sink still turns it on",
      "Digital temperature control in 1-degree steps",
      "Limited lifetime warranty when professionally installed for residential use"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs 3 x 40 A double-pole breakers and draws 112.5 A, too much for many panels",
      "No flow restriction when overloaded, so the temperature drops instead"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "One of the best-known whole-house electric tankless units, and the value pick wherever the panel can spare 112.5 amps and the groundwater is not icy.",
    "description": "The ECO 27 is the unit most people have heard of, and for a whole-house electric tankless it is fairly priced. It uses three 9 kW elements for 27 kW total and modulates its power to the flow rate, so a hand-wash at a sink does not fire all three elements at full output. EcoSmart's own sizing chart, based on 105°F output, puts it at about 5.2 GPM with 70°F incoming water, 4.2 GPM at 60°F, 3.4 GPM at 50°F and 2.8 GPM at 40°F. That is two showers in a warm climate and roughly one in a cold one. Electrical requirements are where you need to stop and check. EcoSmart specifies three 40 amp double-pole breakers, three runs of 8 AWG wire and a 112.5 amp draw. On a 200 amp service that is more than half the panel, and an electrician has to confirm the rest of the house still fits. When you ask for more hot water than it can make, the ECO 27 does not restrict flow the way a Stiebel Eltron Plus does, so the temperature drifts down. The lifetime warranty is a genuine plus, but read the terms; EcoSmart ties it to installation by a licensed professional."
  },
  {
    "id": "stiebel-eltron-tempra-29-plus",
    "name": "Stiebel Eltron Tempra 29 Plus",
    "category": "Whole-House Electric Tankless, 28.8 kW",
    "badge": "Best for Cooler Climates",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 450,
    "pros": [
      "28.8 kW at 240 V with the same Advanced Flow Control as the Tempra 24 Plus",
      "Stiebel rates an 87°F rise at 2.25 GPM and a 66°F rise at 3.0 GPM",
      "About 3 GPM at 105°F from 40°F inlet water by the energy math",
      "7-year leakage and 3-year parts warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs 3 x 40 A double-pole breakers, a 120 A draw",
      "Output falls by about a quarter on 208 V service (21.6 kW)"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The step up from the Tempra 24 Plus for colder groundwater or a second simultaneous shower, if your panel can supply three 40 amp circuits.",
    "description": "The Tempra 29 Plus is the model we would choose for a two-bathroom house where winter groundwater falls into the 40s or 50s. It adds about 5 kW over the Tempra 24 Plus, and Stiebel Eltron's rating table shows the difference clearly: an 87°F temperature rise at 2.25 GPM and a 66°F rise at 3.0 GPM on 240 volts. In practical terms, from 40°F inlet water to a 105°F shower, it delivers roughly 3 GPM, which is one full shower plus a tap, or two low-flow showerheads. You keep Advanced Flow Control, so if someone starts the dishwasher mid-shower the unit slows the flow slightly rather than letting the water turn lukewarm. The cost is electrical. The Tempra 29 Plus needs three 40 amp double-pole breakers and draws 120 amps at full output, which puts it in the same panel conversation as the ECO 27 and ahead of the Tempra 24 Plus. Homes on 208 volt service, common in some apartments and condos, should note that output drops to 21.6 kW. Stiebel's 7-year leakage and 3-year parts warranty is shorter than EcoSmart's lifetime coverage, but you get flow control in exchange."
  },
  {
    "id": "ecosmart-eco-36",
    "name": "EcoSmart ECO 36",
    "category": "Whole-House Electric Tankless, 36 kW",
    "badge": "Most Output for Cold Groundwater",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.1,
    "reviewCount": 730,
    "pros": [
      "36 kW, the largest residential size EcoSmart makes",
      "EcoSmart says it heats nearly 3.5 GPM from 37°F groundwater",
      "Up to about 6 GPM in warmer climates",
      "Self-modulating, with a digital setpoint in 1-degree steps",
      "Limited lifetime warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs 4 x 40 A double-pole breakers and draws 150 A, which usually means a service upgrade",
      "Overloads by dropping temperature, with no flow restriction"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The electric tankless to consider when cold groundwater rules out everything smaller, but only after an electrician confirms the service can carry 150 amps of water heater.",
    "description": "If you live where groundwater is genuinely cold and you still want electric tankless, 36 kW is roughly the minimum for two simultaneous showers, and the ECO 36 is the most widely sold unit at that size. EcoSmart says it heats nearly 3.5 GPM from 37°F groundwater and up to about 6 GPM in warmer climates. Our own check of the energy math agrees: 36 kW lifting water from 40°F to 105°F works out to a little under 4 GPM. That is a real whole-house number in a cold climate, and it is what separates this unit from the 27 kW class. The problem is the electrical bill of materials. The ECO 36 needs four 40 amp double-pole breakers, four runs of 8 AWG wire and a 150 amp draw. On a 200 amp service, that leaves very little for a range, dryer, heat pump and everything else, and many houses need a 300 or 400 amp upgrade before this unit is legal to install. Price that upgrade before you price the heater. Like the ECO 27, it lets the temperature fall when overloaded instead of slowing the flow, and its reviews are more mixed than the smaller models."
  },
  {
    "id": "rheem-rtex-18",
    "name": "Rheem RTEX-18",
    "category": "Small-Home Electric Tankless, 18 kW",
    "badge": "Best for Small Homes and Warm Climates",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.2,
    "reviewCount": 2100,
    "pros": [
      "18 kW from two heating chambers, on 2 x 40 A breakers (75 A)",
      "Rheem rates a 62°F rise at 2.0 GPM and a 41°F rise at 3.0 GPM",
      "Field-replaceable copper immersion elements",
      "Digital thermostat from 80°F to 140°F in 1-degree steps, 0.3 GPM turn-on"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only about 1.9 GPM from 40°F water, not a whole-house heater in the North",
      "5-year heating chamber and 1-year parts warranty is the shortest here"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A sensible whole-house heater for a small home or apartment in a warm climate, and the electric tankless that asks least of your panel.",
    "description": "Not every house needs 27 kW. In a warm-climate condo, a one-bathroom bungalow or an accessory dwelling unit with low-flow fixtures, the 18 kW Rheem RTEX-18 is often enough, and it needs far less electrical capacity than the units above it. Rheem specifies two 40 amp breakers, 8 AWG copper wire and a 75 amp draw at 240 volts. Its rating table shows a 62°F temperature rise at 2.0 GPM and a 41°F rise at 3.0 GPM, so with 70°F incoming water it can feed one shower and a sink at once, and with 60°F water it runs a single shower comfortably. From 40°F groundwater it manages only about 1.9 GPM at 105°F, which is why we would not put it in a northern home as the only water heater. The design is straightforward: two heating chambers with copper immersion elements that can be replaced individually, bottom 3/4-inch connections and a digital thermostat adjustable from 80°F to 140°F. The weak spot is coverage. Rheem warrants the heating chamber for five years and parts for one, well short of EcoSmart's lifetime warranty. Pick it for its modest electrical demands, not for longevity promises."
  },
  {
    "id": "ecosmart-eco-11",
    "name": "EcoSmart ECO 11",
    "category": "Point-of-Use Electric Tankless, 13 kW",
    "badge": "Best Point-of-Use",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 40,
    "pros": [
      "Compact 11.5 x 8 x 3.75 inch unit fits under a sink or in a vanity",
      "13 kW at 240 V on a single 60 A breaker and 6 AWG wire",
      "EcoSmart's chart shows 2.34 GPM at 67°F inlet and 1.31 GPM at 37°F, with 105°F output",
      "Activates at 0.25 GPM, so it works with low-flow aerators",
      "Limited lifetime warranty when professionally installed"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "One shower in a warm climate at most; not a whole-house heater",
      "Still needs a dedicated 240 V, 60 A circuit, not a plug-in outlet"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The point-of-use unit to buy for a remote bathroom, kitchen or tank-booster, as long as you run a proper 240 volt circuit to it.",
    "description": "Point-of-use is where electric tankless makes the most sense, and the ECO 11 is the unit we would start with. It mounts under a sink or in a vanity, measures about 11.5 by 8 by 3.75 inches, and sits next to the fixture it feeds, which ends the long wait for hot water at a bathroom at the far end of the house. Despite the name, it is a 13 kW heater on 240 volts, with two 5.5 kW elements, a 54 amp draw, a single 60 amp breaker and 6 AWG wire. EcoSmart's chart, based on 105°F output, shows 2.34 GPM with 67°F incoming water and 1.31 GPM at 37°F. That covers a kitchen sink or two bathroom sinks almost anywhere, and a single low-flow shower in a warm climate. It switches on at 0.25 GPM, which matters with modern low-flow aerators that cheaper units sometimes fail to detect. The limits are honest ones. It is not a whole-house heater, and it is not a plug-in appliance; you need a dedicated 240 volt circuit. EcoSmart's lifetime warranty requires installation by a licensed professional for residential use."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Electric Tankless Water Heaters",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-electric-tankless-water-heater/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best electric tankless water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most one- or two-bathroom homes in moderate or warm climates, the Stiebel Eltron Tempra 24 Plus, because its Advanced Flow Control keeps water hot when demand runs high and it needs only two 50 amp breakers. The EcoSmart ECO 27 is the better value if your panel can supply three 40 amp circuits and 112.5 amps."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size breaker does an electric tankless water heater need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the model. Whole-house units use several double-pole breakers: the Tempra 24 Plus needs two 50 amp, the ECO 27 and Tempra 29 Plus need three 40 amp, and the ECO 36 needs four 40 amp. A point-of-use ECO 11 needs a single 60 amp breaker. Always follow the manufacturer's specification."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a 200 amp service handle an electric tankless water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Often, but not always. A 200 amp service is not 200 spare amps, and a 27 kW heater alone draws about 112 amps. An electrician's load calculation, counting your range, dryer, heating, cooling and any EV charger, decides whether it fits. Whole-house 36 kW units frequently require a service upgrade first."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many GPM do I need from a tankless water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Add the fixtures you run at once. A shower is typically 1.8 to 2.5 GPM and a kitchen tap about 1.5, so a shower plus a tap is around 4 GPM. Then check what the heater delivers at your coldest incoming water temperature, which in northern states may be in the 40s."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do electric tankless water heaters work in cold climates?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, but output drops sharply with cold groundwater. With 40°F incoming water, a 27 kW unit delivers under 3 GPM at shower temperature, roughly one shower. Cold-climate homes need 29 to 36 kW for two showers, which requires a lot of electrical capacity, so a heat pump or gas unit is often the better fit."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is electric tankless cheaper to run than a tank?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Somewhat, because it does not keep a tank hot around the clock, but the savings over a modern insulated electric tank are modest. A heat pump water heater is much cheaper to run than either, typically using a third to a quarter of the electricity, though it needs more space and a suitable location."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does an electric tankless water heater last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "With maintenance, often well over a decade, since there is no tank to corrode. Elements, flow sensors and thermal cutouts are the usual failures, and on many models elements can be replaced individually. Hard water shortens life, so clean the inlet screen and descale on the manufacturer's schedule, or fit a softener."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "best electric tankless water heater",
      "electric tankless water heater",
      "whole house electric tankless water heater",
      "point of use tankless water heater",
      "electric tankless water heater breaker size",
      "stiebel eltron tempra vs ecosmart"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            6 Heaters Compared
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Electric Tankless Water Heaters</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Whole-house and point-of-use electric tankless heaters compared on what decides whether they work: flow at your groundwater temperature and the amps your panel can spare.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#stiebel-eltron-tempra-24"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            How to Choose
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#honest-case" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The Honest Case</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#groundwater" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Groundwater Temperature</a>
          <a href="#electrical" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Breakers and Panel Capacity</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. How to Size One</a>
          <a href="#flow-control" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Modulation and Flow Control</a>
          <a href="#whole-house-vs-point-of-use" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Whole-House vs Point-of-Use</a>
          <a href="#running-costs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Running Costs and Alternatives</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Installation and Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          An electric tankless water heater can give you endless hot water with no venting and no gas line, but only if two numbers line up: how cold your water is in January and how many amps your service panel can spare. A 27 kW whole-house unit draws about 112 amps and still delivers under 3 gallons per minute from 40°F groundwater. Get those two numbers right and electric tankless is a tidy, reliable choice. Get them wrong and you end up with a lukewarm shower or a service upgrade you did not budget for.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric tankless heaters are easy to compare badly. Every box promises endless hot water, and the headline flow rates are measured with warm incoming water that most of the country never sees in winter. So we ignored the maximum GPM on the packaging and compared each heater on three things that decide whether it will actually work in your house: how much it can raise cold water at a realistic flow, how much electrical capacity it consumes, and what happens when you ask it for more than it can make.
          </p>

          <p>
            We built the list from the manufacturers' own spec sheets, rating tables and installation requirements, and checked the flow figures against the basic physics of heating water, which no brand can get around. One kilowatt of electric heat raises one gallon per minute by about 6.8°F. A 27 kW unit therefore lifts roughly 184°F at 1 GPM, or about 61°F at 3 GPM. When a brand's chart matched that math, we used it. When we had to calculate a figure ourselves, the product description says so.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electrical demand counted as heavily as heating power. A heater that needs 150 amps is not a fair comparison with one that needs 75, because in many homes the larger one requires a service upgrade that costs more than the heater. We also favoured units with good overload behaviour, real temperature control rather than a dial, simple element replacement, and warranties that cover the heating chamber for years rather than months.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not include gas units here, because gas tankless is a different decision with different installation costs; our main <a href="/best-tankless-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">tankless water heater rankings</a> cover both fuels side by side. The six heaters below are all 240 volt electric models from established brands with long sales histories, so parts, elements and support are available. Every rating and review count is the Amazon figure at the time of writing, rounded down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="honest-case" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Honest Case For and Against Electric Tankless
          </h2>

          <p>
            The case for electric tankless is real. There is no venting, no gas line, no combustion and no flue to inspect. The units are small enough to hang on a closet wall, and nearly all the electricity becomes heat in the water, which is why makers quote efficiency above 99 percent. There is no tank losing heat around the clock. For a cabin, a small apartment, an addition or a remote bathroom, those advantages make electric tankless the simplest way to get hot water.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case against is physics and wiring. Heating water instantly takes an enormous amount of power, and a whole-house electric unit draws as much current at full output as the rest of a typical home combined. A 27 kW heater pulls about 112 amps at 240 volts. That is why the first question about electric tankless is never which brand, but whether your service panel can supply it, and why the second is how cold your water is in January.
          </p>

          <p>
            Efficiency also needs context. An electric tankless converts electricity to heat at close to 100 percent, but a heat pump water heater moves heat from the air rather than making it and typically uses a third to a quarter of the electricity for the same hot water. If your goal is lower bills rather than unlimited showers or saving space, read our <a href="/best-heat-pump-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">heat pump water heater guide</a> before you commit. The trade-offs in our <a href="/tankless-water-heater-pros-and-cons/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">tankless pros and cons</a> guide apply here too.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our view is simple. Electric tankless is an excellent choice for point-of-use, for small homes in warm climates, and for houses that already have generous electrical service. It is a poor choice for a large family in a cold climate on a 100 or 150 amp panel, where the right answer is usually a heat pump water heater or a gas unit. The rest of this guide gives you the numbers to tell which group you are in.
          </p>
        </div>

