        <div id="groundwater" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Groundwater Temperature Decides Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every electric tankless has a fixed amount of heat to give, so the flow it can deliver depends entirely on how far it has to raise the water. That distance is the temperature rise: the outlet temperature you want minus the temperature of the water coming in. Most people shower at around 100°F to 105°F. If your incoming water is 70°F, the heater only needs a 35°F rise. If it is 40°F, it needs a 65°F rise, and the same heater delivers barely half the flow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Incoming water temperature varies far more than people expect. In much of the northern United States, winter groundwater sits in the 40s, and in some places dips into the high 30s. Across the middle of the country it is commonly in the 50s. In Florida, the Gulf Coast and the desert Southwest it can be 70°F or warmer. Size for the coldest month, not the average, because January is when an undersized unit leaves someone with a cold rinse.
          </p>

