        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Size One for Your House
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the coldest incoming water you will see. You can check it yourself in winter by running a cold tap for a minute and putting a kitchen thermometer in a glass, or ask your water utility, which often publishes source water temperatures. Subtract that figure from 105°F. That is your design temperature rise, and it matters more than the number of bathrooms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, decide how much hot water you need at once. List the fixtures that realistically run together in your house at the busiest moment, usually a shower plus a sink, or two showers on a school morning. Use the flow printed on the showerhead or tap aerator where you can find it. Then add them up. Remember that a shower is a mix of hot and cold water, so the heater supplies most but not all of the shower's flow at 105°F.
          </p>

          <p>
            Divide the heater's kilowatts by your temperature rise and multiply by 6.8 to get its flow. A 27 kW unit with a 55°F rise gives about 3.3 GPM. If that number covers your simultaneous demand, the size works. If it falls short, you either step up a size, accept that two showers will not run together in winter, or switch to a unit with flow control so the shortfall shows up as lower pressure rather than cold water. Our <a href="/what-size-tankless-water-heater-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">full sizing walkthrough</a> has worked examples.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid the opposite mistake as well. Oversizing a whole-house electric unit buys flow you will rarely use while consuming electrical capacity you may need later for a heat pump or an EV charger. A couple in a warm climate with low-flow fixtures does not need 36 kW. The Rheem RTEX-18 or the Tempra 24 Plus will do, and each leaves far more of the panel free.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flow-control" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Self-Modulation and Flow Control Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every heater on this list self-modulates. A flow sensor reads how much water is moving, the controller reads the inlet temperature and setpoint, and the unit sends only as much power to the elements as that flow needs. This is what keeps the outlet temperature steady when you nudge the tap, and it is why a hand-wash at a sink does not fire the full 27 or 36 kW. Modulation does not make a heater more efficient at turning electricity into heat, but it does stop overshooting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Modulation cannot create capacity. When demand exceeds the heater's maximum, something has to give, and there are two possible outcomes. Most units, including the EcoSmart and Rheem models here, keep the flow you asked for and let the temperature fall. The water stays warm but not hot, and the person in the shower notices. Stiebel Eltron's Plus models take the other route with Advanced Flow Control: a motorized valve reduces the flow until the heater can hold the setpoint, so the water stays hot and runs a little lighter.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a house where demand occasionally exceeds capacity, which is most houses with electric tankless in a cold month, flow control is the better failure. A slightly weaker shower is an annoyance. A shower that drops ten degrees when the washing machine starts is a complaint. That single behaviour is the main reason a Tempra Plus ranks above a cheaper unit with more kilowatts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Minimum activation flow matters in the other direction. A tankless heater only turns on above a threshold flow, around 0.25 to 0.3 GPM for the EcoSmart and Rheem units and higher for some Stiebel models. With modern low-flow bathroom aerators running at 0.5 GPM or less, a heater with a high threshold can fail to switch on at a barely open tap. If you have low-flow fixtures, check the activation figure and open the tap a little further before assuming the heater is faulty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whole-house-vs-point-of-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Whole-House vs Point-of-Use
          </h2>

          <p>
            A whole-house unit replaces your water heater. It is installed where the tank used to be, feeds every fixture through the existing pipes, and needs the large electrical service described above. A point-of-use unit sits beside one fixture or one bathroom, under a sink or inside a vanity, and heats only the water for that location. It needs one circuit rather than three or four, and it delivers hot water within seconds because the pipe run is a few feet long.
          </p>

          <p>
            Point-of-use is where electric tankless is most clearly the right answer. A remote bathroom over a garage, a kitchen far from the water heater, a basement bar, a detached workshop or a guest suite are all good candidates. Each avoids the long wait for hot water and the gallons of cold water run down the drain while waiting. The ECO 11 on our list is the classic example, and it fits a single 60 amp circuit.
          </p>

          <p>
            A third approach uses a point-of-use unit as a booster. Installed after an existing tank, it tops up the temperature when the tank runs low, which extends the time before the shower goes cold without replacing the tank. It is a cheap way to stretch an undersized water heater in a house where a second bathroom has been added.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small 120 volt units exist and are sold for hand sinks, but they are limited to around 3.5 kW and a fraction of a gallon per minute at a useful temperature rise. They are fine for a hand-wash sink in an office or a shop. They will not run a shower or a kitchen sink, and buyers who expect them to are the source of most of their bad reviews.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Running Costs and the Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            An electric tankless heater does not waste energy keeping a tank hot, so its running cost is essentially the cost of heating the water you actually use. That is lower than an electric tank, which loses some heat through its walls around the clock, but the difference is modest for most households because modern tanks are well insulated. The big savings people expect from tankless usually come from replacing an old, poorly insulated tank, not from the technology.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compared with a heat pump water heater, electric tankless costs more to run. Both use electricity, but a heat pump unit moves heat from the surrounding air and typically delivers three to four units of heat for each unit of electricity. Electric tankless delivers one. For a family using a lot of hot water, the heat pump's running cost is the lowest of any electric option, although it needs space, a drain and a room that can tolerate being cooled. Our <a href="/tankless-water-heater-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">tankless water heater cost breakdown</a> compares upfront and running costs across types.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas tankless is the other comparison. It produces far more heat than any residential electric unit, so it delivers much higher flow from cold groundwater, but it needs a gas line large enough for its input and proper venting. Condensing gas units vent through plastic pipe and recover heat from the exhaust; non-condensing units need stainless venting. If you have gas available, our <a href="/condensing-vs-non-condensing-tankless-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">condensing vs non-condensing guide</a> explains the choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some utilities bill residential customers for peak demand as well as energy used, and a 27 or 36 kW heater can set a high peak in a few minutes of showering. Most residential tariffs do not, but check yours before you install a large unit. If you plan to run your water heater from backup power during outages, note that an electric tankless is far beyond what a portable generator can supply, as our guide to <a href="/can-a-portable-generator-power-an-electric-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">running a water heater on a generator</a> explains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Installation, Maintenance and Troubleshooting
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installation is electrical work first and plumbing second. The plumbing is simple, two 3/4-inch connections on most whole-house units and smaller fittings on point-of-use models, but the circuits, breakers, wire and load calculation need a licensed electrician, and most manufacturers tie their warranties to professional installation. Fit isolation valves on the inlet and outlet so the unit can be serviced or flushed without draining the house, and install it where a leak will be noticed and can drain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hard water is the main enemy of any tankless heater. Scale builds on the elements and in the heating chambers, reduces heat transfer and eventually burns out an element. Clean the inlet screen periodically and descale according to the manufacturer's schedule, more often in hard water areas. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-flush-a-tankless-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how to flush a tankless water heater</a> covers the process. A water softener or scale-inhibiting filter ahead of the heater can extend element life considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric tankless units have fewer parts than gas models: no igniter, no fan, no flue. When something fails it is usually an element, a flow sensor, a thermal cutout or a breaker. Units with field-replaceable elements, like the EcoSmart and Rheem models, are cheap to repair, and a replacement element is a common spare to keep on hand. Expect a long service life with care; our article on <a href="/how-long-do-tankless-water-heaters-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how long tankless water heaters last</a> has realistic ranges.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the water runs warm but never hot, the cause is usually one tripped breaker, a failed element, or simply demand beyond the unit's capacity on a cold day. If it does not heat at all, check the breakers, the flow rate at the tap and the inlet screen. Our troubleshooting guide for a <a href="/tankless-water-heater-not-heating/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">tankless water heater not heating</a> goes step by step. And because a slow leak at a fitting inside a closet can soak drywall for weeks before anyone notices, a quick check with one of the <a href="/best-moisture-meter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best moisture meters</a> after installation is cheap insurance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing From the Box Flow Rate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The big GPM figure on the box is measured with warm incoming water. In a northern winter the same heater may deliver half that. Size from your coldest incoming water temperature and the manufacturer's rating table, and assume the worst month, not the average, decides whether a second shower runs hot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Before the Load Calculation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 27 kW unit draws about 112 amps, and a 36 kW unit 150. Many 200 amp services cannot add that without an upgrade that costs more than the heater. Get an electrician's load calculation first, then choose the heater the panel can actually carry, not the other way round.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a 120 V Unit to Run a Shower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small 120 volt point-of-use heaters are around 3.5 kW and suit a hand-wash sink. They cannot heat shower or kitchen flow to a useful temperature. Showers and kitchen sinks need a 240 volt unit with roughly 13 kW or more, and its own dedicated circuit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Hard Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Scale is the main reason electric tankless elements fail early. In hard water, clean the inlet screen and descale on the maker's schedule, and consider a softener or scale-inhibiting filter. A heater that is never maintained in hard water can lose an element in a few years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Oversizing Just in Case</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buying 36 kW for a small home in a warm climate wastes money and ties up panel capacity you may want later for a heat pump or EV charger. Match the heater to realistic simultaneous demand and your groundwater temperature, and leave headroom in the panel instead of in the water heater.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best electric tankless water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most one- or two-bathroom homes in moderate or warm climates, the Stiebel Eltron Tempra 24 Plus, because its Advanced Flow Control keeps water hot when demand runs high and it needs only two 50 amp breakers. The EcoSmart ECO 27 is the better value if your panel can supply three 40 amp circuits and 112.5 amps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size breaker does an electric tankless water heater need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the model. Whole-house units use several double-pole breakers: the Tempra 24 Plus needs two 50 amp, the ECO 27 and Tempra 29 Plus need three 40 amp, and the ECO 36 needs four 40 amp. A point-of-use ECO 11 needs a single 60 amp breaker. Always follow the manufacturer's specification.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 200 amp service handle an electric tankless water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, but not always. A 200 amp service is not 200 spare amps, and a 27 kW heater alone draws about 112 amps. An electrician's load calculation, counting your range, dryer, heating, cooling and any EV charger, decides whether it fits. Whole-house 36 kW units frequently require a service upgrade first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many GPM do I need from a tankless water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add the fixtures you run at once. A shower is typically 1.8 to 2.5 GPM and a kitchen tap about 1.5, so a shower plus a tap is around 4 GPM. Then check what the heater delivers at your coldest incoming water temperature, which in northern states may be in the 40s.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do electric tankless water heaters work in cold climates?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but output drops sharply with cold groundwater. With 40°F incoming water, a 27 kW unit delivers under 3 GPM at shower temperature, roughly one shower. Cold-climate homes need 29 to 36 kW for two showers, which requires a lot of electrical capacity, so a heat pump or gas unit is often the better fit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is electric tankless cheaper to run than a tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Somewhat, because it does not keep a tank hot around the clock, but the savings over a modern insulated electric tank are modest. A heat pump water heater is much cheaper to run than either, typically using a third to a quarter of the electricity, though it needs more space and a suitable location.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does an electric tankless water heater last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With maintenance, often well over a decade, since there is no tank to corrode. Elements, flow sensors and thermal cutouts are the usual failures, and on many models elements can be replaced individually. Hard water shortens life, so clean the inlet screen and descale on the manufacturer's schedule, or fit a softener.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

